# 2D Enemy Patrol and Chase AI

A simple enemy can move between waypoints and chase a target inside a range. This sample uses Rigidbody2D and Transform positions for target detection; it does not implement line-of-sight occlusion.

## Setup
Create an enemy with Rigidbody2D and Collider2D. Assign two waypoint Transforms and a target. Freeze Z rotation if the enemy should stay upright. Choose whether the enemy moves by physics or by Transform; this example uses Rigidbody2D.MovePosition.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class EnemyPatrolChase2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointA;
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointB;
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float patrolSpeed = 2f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float chaseSpeed = 3f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float detectionRange = 5f;

        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private bool towardB = true;

        private void Awake() => body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            Vector2 current = body.position;
            bool chasing = target != null &&
                ((Vector2)target.position - current).sqrMagnitude <= detectionRange * detectionRange;

            Vector2 destination;
            float speed;
            if (chasing)
            {
                destination = target.position;
                speed = chaseSpeed;
            }
            else
            {
                if (pointA == null || pointB == null) return;
                destination = towardB ? (Vector2)pointB.position : (Vector2)pointA.position;
                speed = patrolSpeed;
            }

            Vector2 next = Vector2.MoveTowards(current, destination, speed * Time.fixedDeltaTime);
            body.MovePosition(next);

            if (!chasing && Vector2.Distance(next, destination) <= 0.05f)
                towardB = !towardB;
        }
    }

## Extensions
Add a line-of-sight raycast for walls, a return-to-patrol delay, attack range and cooldown, and explicit states when behavior becomes complex. Do not allow a separate movement script to also move the same Rigidbody2D.

## Common mistakes
- Waypoints are missing.
- Rigidbody2D settings conflict with direct Transform changes elsewhere.
- Chase range is checked using distance from the wrong origin.
- Enemy chases through walls because only distance is checked.

# 2D Sprite Animation and Animator

## Import and create clips
1. Set sprite sheet Texture Type to Sprite (2D and UI), Sprite Mode to Multiple, then Apply.
2. Open Sprite Editor, slice the sheet, then Apply.
3. Select sliced sprites in order and create an animation clip.
4. Keep Pixels Per Unit and sprite pivots consistent to prevent size changes and wobble.
5. Inspect Animation window keyframes and remove accidental keys on properties the clip should not control.

## Animator setup
Add Animator and assign an Animator Controller. Create parameters such as Speed (Float), Grounded (Bool), and Jump (Trigger). Use explicit transition conditions. Disable Has Exit Time for immediate locomotion changes; enable it when a clip should finish first. A Blend Tree can smoothly blend idle/walk/run. Names and types must match exactly.

## Parameter driver
Save as SpriteAnimatorDriver.cs and attach to the Animator object.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class SpriteAnimatorDriver : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Animator animator;
        private static readonly int SpeedHash = Animator.StringToHash("Speed");
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (animator == null) animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
        }
        public void SetMoveSpeed(float speed)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetFloat(SpeedHash, Mathf.Abs(speed));
        }
        public void SetGrounded(bool grounded)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetBool("Grounded", grounded);
        }
        public void TriggerJump()
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetTrigger("Jump");
        }
    }

This drives parameters; it does not create states or transitions. Configure those in the Animator window.

## Sorting and troubleshooting
SpriteRenderer uses Sorting Layer and Order in Layer. For top-down games, sorting by Y may be useful. Check for missing controllers, misspelled parameters, wrong parameter types, disabled Loop Time, inconsistent pivots, and Animator/gameplay code both writing the same Transform property.

# 2D Sprite Flipping and Aiming

## Flip a character horizontally
Use SpriteRenderer.flipX when only the rendered sprite needs to face the other direction. This avoids changing Transform scale and introducing negative-scale side effects.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class SpriteFacing2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private SpriteRenderer spriteRenderer;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float deadZone = 0.05f;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null) spriteRenderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();
        }

        public void SetHorizontalMove(float x)
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null || Mathf.Abs(x) < deadZone) return;
            spriteRenderer.flipX = x < 0f;
        }
    }

Call SetHorizontalMove with the character's horizontal movement input or velocity. If the character has a weapon/child transform that must also face the aim direction, flip or rotate that transform separately.

## Aim toward mouse
For a 2D world on the XY plane and a standard camera facing that plane, convert the mouse to world coordinates. Use camera-space depth and check the returned coordinate plane. A simple screen-to-world conversion must use a depth that intersects the gameplay plane.

    Vector3 mouse = Camera.main.ScreenToWorldPoint(
        new Vector3(Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().x,
                    Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().y,
                    Mathf.Abs(Camera.main.transform.position.z)));
    Vector2 aim = (Vector2)mouse - (Vector2)weapon.position;
    if (aim.sqrMagnitude > 0.0001f)
        weapon.right = aim.normalized;

This snippet assumes a conventional 2D camera at negative Z and gameplay around Z = 0. It requires the Input System namespace and a Mouse device. For gamepad aiming, use a direction vector from the stick instead.

## Common mistakes
- Sprite is mirrored but colliders/child weapon are not positioned as intended.
- Transform scale is negated and creates hierarchy side effects.
- Mouse depth does not intersect the gameplay plane.
- Code rotates the whole Rigidbody root and changes physics behavior.

# 2D Audio: Music and Sound Effects

AudioListener is usually on the camera; keep one active listener. AudioSource plays clips and configures volume, pitch, looping, spatial blend, and mixer routing. AudioMixer groups sources and controls effects/volume centrally. For conventional 2D effects, set Spatial Blend to 0.

## One-shot service
Create an AudioService object, add an AudioSource, disable Play On Awake, and assign a short confirm clip. Save this as AudioService.cs.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class AudioService : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public static AudioService Instance { get; private set; }
        [SerializeField] private AudioSource source;
        [SerializeField] private AudioClip confirmClip;
        [SerializeField, Range(0f, 1f)] private float volume = 1f;
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (Instance != null && Instance != this)
            {
                Destroy(gameObject);
                return;
            }
            Instance = this;
            if (source == null) source = GetComponent<AudioSource>();
        }
        public void PlayConfirm()
        {
            if (source != null && confirmClip != null)
                source.PlayOneShot(confirmClip, volume);
        }
    }

Call AudioService.Instance?.PlayConfirm() from UI/gameplay code. This sample does not persist across scenes. If adding DontDestroyOnLoad, prevent duplicates and deliberately manage lifetime.

## Import and performance
Short effects may use Decompress On Load or Compressed In Memory depending on latency, memory, and CPU. Long music often suits streaming or compressed-in-memory settings. Profile on target hardware. Pool or cap sources for rapid repeated sounds.

Common mistakes: no listener, multiple listeners, no clip/play call, Spatial Blend incorrectly set, mixer muted, or playback occurring while audio is paused.

# 2D Camera Bounds and Level Confinement

A follow camera should stay inside level bounds. A simple clamp computes the camera's orthographic half-height and half-width, then limits its center position so the view stays inside the rectangle.

## Clamp an orthographic camera
Attach to the Camera or camera rig. Assign a target and world-space minimum/maximum bounds. Assumes a 2D orthographic camera and rectangular level bounds.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Camera))]
    public sealed class CameraBounds2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 minBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 maxBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset;
        private Camera cam;

        private void Awake() => cam = GetComponent<Camera>();

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null || !cam.orthographic) return;

            float halfHeight = cam.orthographicSize;
            float halfWidth = halfHeight * cam.aspect;

            float minX = minBounds.x + halfWidth;
            float maxX = maxBounds.x - halfWidth;
            float minY = minBounds.y + halfHeight;
            float maxY = maxBounds.y - halfHeight;

            // When the view is wider/taller than the level, center it on that axis.
            float x = minX > maxX ? (minBounds.x + maxBounds.x) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.x + offset.x, minX, maxX);
            float y = minY > maxY ? (minBounds.y + maxBounds.y) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.y + offset.y, minY, maxY);

            transform.position = new Vector3(x, y, transform.position.z);
        }
    }

Set min/max to the actual world-space rectangle around the playable level, not the player's bounds. If using a separate smooth follow script, combine follow and clamping in one controller or ensure update order is intentional. Cinemachine Confiner may be preferable for irregular level boundaries.

## Common mistakes
- Using pixel bounds instead of world coordinates.
- Forgetting that camera half-width depends on aspect ratio.
- Clamping before smoothing, then smoothing moves the camera outside the bounds.
- The level is smaller than the camera view, requiring centering rather than an impossible clamp.

# 2D Camera: Follow, Framing, and Pixel Art

For an orthographic camera, Size is half the vertical world-space view height. Size 5 means 10 world units high; visible width depends on aspect ratio.

## Smooth follow
Save as CameraFollow2D.cs and attach to Main Camera. Assign the player Transform. A common convention is gameplay at Z = 0 and camera at Z = -10.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class CameraFollow2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float smoothTime = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset = new Vector2(0f, 1f);
        [SerializeField] private bool followX = true;
        [SerializeField] private bool followY = true;
        private Vector3 velocity;
        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null) return;
            Vector3 current = transform.position;
            Vector3 desired = new Vector3(
                followX ? target.position.x + offset.x : current.x,
                followY ? target.position.y + offset.y : current.y,
                current.z);
            transform.position = Vector3.SmoothDamp(current, desired, ref velocity, smoothTime);
        }
    }

LateUpdate is a common place for camera following. For physics-driven targets, test interpolation and consider Cinemachine.

## Pixel art checklist
- Use consistent Pixels Per Unit.
- Consider the 2D Pixel Perfect package/component.
- Match reference resolution and art PPU.
- Avoid fractional camera motion if crisp pixel alignment is required.
- Test target resolutions and aspect ratios.

A follow script does not constrain the camera to level bounds. Use Cinemachine confiners or calculate bounds using camera half-width and half-height. Use a separate rig/offset for shake so shake and follow do not fight.

Common mistakes: missing target, wrong Z convention, confusing Size with full view height, excessive smoothing, and sub-pixel blur.
